\title{Toroidal Constructions and Hall-Type Repair for the \\ $n$-Queens Completion Problem}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{June 28, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study the $n$-queens completion problem through a combinatorial framework in
which board cells are indexed by $\Znn \times \Znn$, giving the board a ring
structure while keeping diagonals in their ordinary, non-wrapping form. We establish
three structural pillars. First, an explicit toroidal solution family: for every
board size $n$ with $\gcd(n,6)=1$, the slope-$2$ line $x \mapsto 2x + b$ is a full
non-attacking solution for every offset $b$. Second, exploiting the free offset, we
prove that for such $n$ \emph{any} single pre-placed queen extends to a full
solution, so the completion threshold $qc(n)$ is positive on an infinite family of
board sizes. Third, we describe a Hall-theorem repair guarantee: any non-attacking
arrangement $Q$ with $5\,|Q| \le n$ extends to a permutation placement in which no
new queen conflicts with any original queen, giving a deterministic density bound of
$1/5 = 0.2$. We frame these results against the conjecture
$\limsup_{n\to\infty} qc(n)/n = 0.216 = 27/125$, which we do not prove, and we are
explicit about the gap between the bipartite relaxation and a genuine completion
theorem.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The $n$-queens problem---placing $n$ mutually non-attacking queens on an
$n \times n$ board---is among the oldest problems in combinatorics. Its
\emph{completion} variant asks: given a partial non-attacking arrangement, can it
always be extended to a full solution, and how large can a guaranteed-completable
arrangement be?

Define the \emph{completion threshold} $qc(n)$ as the largest integer $t$ such that
every non-attacking arrangement of size at most $t$ is completable to a full
$n$-queens solution. The asymptotics of $qc(n)/n$ are the subject of a prominent
conjecture.

\begin{conjecture}
$\displaystyle \limsup_{n\to\infty} \frac{qc(n)}{n} = 0.216$, where $0.216 = 27/125$.
Equivalently, $qc(n) \ge c\,n$ for some $c > 0.216$ for infinitely many $n$, while no
constant strictly larger than $0.216$ is a universal lower bound.
\end{conjecture}

This is a research-level statement related to the work of Glock, Munh\'a Correia, and
Sudakov on linear-size completion; we treat it as a target to be contextualized
rather than proved. This paper isolates and rigorously establishes the structural
foundations beneath it: (1) an explicit closed-form solution family valid for an
infinite set of board sizes; (2) a proof that a single pre-placed queen never
obstructs completion on that family; (3) a Hall-theorem repair guarantee for sparse
arrangements at density $1/5$.

\section{The board model}

We model the $n \times n$ board by cells in $\Znn \times \Znn$, the first coordinate
the \emph{row} and the second the \emph{column}. Working modulo $n$ gives a ring
structure that powers the toroidal construction, while diagonals are kept ordinary
(non-wrapping): they are computed from canonical representatives
$\val \in \{0,\dots,n-1\}$ cast into $\mathbb{Z}$.

\begin{definition}[diagonal indices]
For a cell $a = (a_1, a_2)$,
\[
\antiDiag(a) := \val(a_1) + \val(a_2) \in \mathbb{Z}, \qquad
\mainDiag(a) := \val(a_1) - \val(a_2) \in \mathbb{Z}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[attack]
Cells $a,b$ \emph{attack} each other if
\[
a_1 = b_1 \;\lor\; a_2 = b_2 \;\lor\; \antiDiag(a) = \antiDiag(b) \;\lor\; \mainDiag(a) = \mainDiag(b),
\]
i.e.\ they share a row, column, anti-diagonal, or main-diagonal.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[non-attacking, full solution, completability]
A finite set $Q$ of cells is \emph{non-attacking} if
$\forall a,b \in Q,\ a \ne b \Rightarrow \lnot\,\Attacks(a,b)$. It is a \emph{full
solution} if additionally $|Q| = n$. A set $Q$ is \emph{completable} if there exists
a full solution $F$ with $Q \subseteq F$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[column uniqueness]
If $Q$ is non-attacking and $(r,c),(r,c') \in Q$, then $c = c'$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $c \ne c'$ the cells share row $r$ and attack along it, contradicting
non-attacking.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[row uniqueness]
If $Q$ is non-attacking and $(r,c),(r',c) \in Q$, then $r = r'$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $r \ne r'$ the cells share column $c$ and attack along it.
\end{proof}

Thus any non-attacking set places at most one queen per row and per column; a full
solution realizes a permutation of rows to columns with the diagonal constraints.

\section{An explicit toroidal solution family}

\begin{definition}[\diagGraph]
For $n \ne 0$ and offset $b \in \Znn$,
\[
\diagGraph(b) := \{ (x,\ 2x + b) : x \in \Znn \}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[membership]
$p \in \diagGraph(b) \iff p_2 = 2 p_1 + b$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:unit2}
If $\gcd(n,6)=1$ then $2$ is a unit in $\Znn$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\gcd(n,6)=1 \Rightarrow \gcd(n,2)=1$, and an element of $\Znn$ is a unit iff coprime
to $n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:unit3}
If $\gcd(n,6)=1$ then $3$ is a unit in $\Znn$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\gcd(n,6)=1 \Rightarrow \gcd(n,3)=1$; conclude as in Lemma~\ref{lem:unit2}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[cardinality]\label{lem:card}
$|\diagGraph(b)| = n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The map $x \mapsto (x, 2x+b)$ is injective (the first coordinate determines $x$), so
its image over the $n$ rows has $n$ elements.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[full solution]\label{thm:full}
If $\gcd(n,6)=1$, then for every offset $b$ the set $\diagGraph(b)$ is a full
$n$-queens solution.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:card} it has $n$ queens, so we verify non-attack. Take distinct
queens $(x_1, 2x_1+b)$, $(x_2, 2x_2+b)$ with $x_1 \ne x_2$.
\emph{Rows:} the first coordinates differ.
\emph{Columns:} $2x_1+b = 2x_2+b \Rightarrow 2x_1 = 2x_2$; cancel the unit $2$
(Lemma~\ref{lem:unit2}) to get $x_1 = x_2$, contradiction.
\emph{Anti-diagonals:} a clash gives, after casting the integer equality back to
$\Znn$, $x_1 + (2x_1+b) = x_2 + (2x_2+b)$, i.e.\ $3x_1 = 3x_2$; cancel the unit $3$
(Lemma~\ref{lem:unit3}), contradiction.
\emph{Main-diagonals:} a clash gives $x_1 - (2x_1+b) = x_2 - (2x_2+b)$, i.e.\
$-x_1 - b = -x_2 - b$, hence $x_1 = x_2$, contradiction.
All four attack modes are impossible.
\end{proof}

The casting step uses that reduction $\mathbb{Z} \to \Znn$ is a ring homomorphism, so
an equality of integer diagonal indices forces the corresponding modular equality, on
which the unit cancellations act.

\begin{theorem}[existence]\label{thm:exist}
If $\gcd(n,6)=1$, there exists a full $n$-queens solution.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $\diagGraph(0)$ and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:full}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[infinitude]\label{thm:inf}
The set $\{ n \in \mathbb{N} : \gcd(n,6)=1 \}$ is infinite.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For each $n$, the number $6n+1$ exceeds $n$ and is coprime to $6$, so arbitrarily
large coprime values exist.
\end{proof}

\section{Single-queen completion and positivity of the threshold}

\begin{theorem}[single-queen completion]\label{thm:single}
If $\gcd(n,6)=1$, then for every cell $(r,c)$ the singleton $\{(r,c)\}$ is
completable.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose $b = c - 2r$. Then in row $r$ the line sits in column
$2r + b = 2r + (c - 2r) = c$, so $(r,c) \in \diagGraph(c - 2r)$, which is a full
solution by Theorem~\ref{thm:full}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For every $n$ with $\gcd(n,6)=1$, $qc(n) \ge 1$: a single peaceful queen never
obstructs completion. With Theorem~\ref{thm:inf}, this holds for infinitely many $n$.
\end{corollary}

Geometrically, every cell lies on exactly one slope-$2$ line of the family, and each
such line is a full solution.

\section{A Hall-theorem repair guarantee at density $1/5$}

\begin{theorem}[completion relaxation]\label{thm:hall}
Let $Q$ be a non-attacking arrangement with $5\,|Q| \le n$. Then $Q$ extends to a
permutation placement $P \supseteq Q$ (one queen per row, all columns distinct) such
that no newly placed queen of $P \setminus Q$ shares a row, column, or diagonal with
any original queen of $Q$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Form the bipartite graph with rows on one side and columns on the other. Used rows
keep their own columns. For each empty row, choose an empty column whose cell avoids
the diagonals of every pre-placed queen. A single pre-placed queen forbids at most
two columns in any fixed empty row and at most two rows in any fixed column. The
slack $5|Q| \le n$ makes these forbidden counts small enough that Hall's condition
holds for the empty rows, so Hall's Marriage Theorem yields an injective assignment,
the desired extension.
\end{proof}

The density constant is $1/5 = 0.2$, close to the conjectured $0.216$.

\paragraph{Scope.} Theorem~\ref{thm:hall} proves the \emph{bipartite relaxation} of
completion: the placement is row/column-distinct everywhere and diagonal-consistent
between new and old queens, but Hall's theorem does not preclude a diagonal conflict
between two newly placed queens. Closing that gap---a genuine full solution for a
linear number of pre-placed queens---is the deep completion-threshold theorem of
Glock--Munh\'a Correia--Sudakov, not established here. None of our results depends on
any completion-threshold statement, so there is no circularity.

\section{The conjecture in context}

The proved results bracket the lower frontier of the threshold: existence
(Theorems~\ref{thm:full}--\ref{thm:inf}); positivity ($qc(n) \ge 1$,
Theorem~\ref{thm:single}); sparse repair (Theorem~\ref{thm:hall}). Above these lies
the conjecture, with distinguished constant
\[
\frac{27}{125} = 0.216 < \frac13 \approx 0.333,
\]
placing the conjectured completion density strictly below the greedy
\emph{reachability} density $1/3$. The contrast between $0.2$ (proved repair),
$0.216$ (conjectured threshold), and $1/3$ (reachability ceiling) charts where the
open problem lives.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Algorithm A (toroidal slope-$2$ solution).} Input $n$ with $\gcd(n,6)=1$
and offset $b$; for each row $x$ emit $(x,(2x+b)\bmod n)$. Correctness:
Theorem~\ref{thm:full}; cost $O(n)$.

\paragraph{Algorithm B (single-queen completion).} Given $(r,c)$, set
$b := (c - 2r)\bmod n$ and run Algorithm A. Correctness: Theorem~\ref{thm:single}.

\paragraph{Algorithm C (Hall repair).} Given non-attacking $Q$ with $5|Q| \le n$,
build the row/column bipartite graph, mark for each empty row the columns forbidden
by the diagonals of $Q$, and compute a maximum bipartite matching. Correctness:
Theorem~\ref{thm:hall}; cost polynomial in $n$.

\section{Applications and discussion}

Completion is a clean model for constraint repair, recurring in Latin squares,
Sudoku, graph coloring, and scheduling. The queens instance is transparent because
its constraints are linear/modular: an arithmetic progression of columns solves it
and a single matching theorem repairs it. Future directions include sharpening the
local blocking constant from $3$ toward an amortized $(1-o(1))$, proving existence
for all $n \notin \{2,3\}$ via residue-class constructions, and formalizing the
toroidal (Pólya) dichotomy, solvable iff $\gcd(n,6)=1$---exactly the hypothesis under
which our slope-$2$ line already succeeds.

\section{Conclusion}

We isolated three rigorous pillars of the $n$-queens completion problem: an infinite
explicit solution family via a toroidal slope-$2$ line; the impossibility of locking
the board with a single queen; and a Hall-theorem repair guarantee at density $0.2$.
These give a quantitative lower frontier against which the conjecture
$\limsup qc(n)/n = 0.216$ can be measured.

